From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 20/22] dndcp: Make guest to host drags reach the drop target
 on GNOME and sway

A guest to host drag failed on GNOME too. The detection window was
mapped below the focused window, so a drag leaving a maximized window
never reached it. Under Mutter the window is now raised above other
windows when it is shown as a drop target, and the check for Mutter from
the previous patch now also runs then. Under any Wayland compositor the
pointer is moved over the window three more times from the main loop.
Mutter did not offer the drag to the window on the first move, but did
on the same move made moments later.

A tiling compositor such as sway floats the window where it chooses,
often in the middle of the output rather than at the edge the pointer
left by, so no drag ever passed over it. Once the window is managed, the
plugin reads where it is and moves the pointer there.

After a guest to host drop, the host keeps the guest mouse's button down
until the pointer next moves over the guest. Mutter ends a drag only
once no device holds the button, and the kernel drops a release from a
uinput device whose button is already up, so the release faked through
uinput did nothing. The guest's drag stayed alive and dropped wherever
the pointer was when the host let go. If the drag is still offered to
the detection window 300 ms after the drop, and has not dropped on it,
the plugin now cancels it with Escape, which the uinput device can now
send. The detection window watches for XdndEnter and XdndDrop itself,
because GTK keeps a drop that has arrived until it has read the value,
and the drop alone cannot tell a running drag from one that has just
dropped. The check runs only under Mutter.

A guest to host drag out of a maximized Nautilus 50.2.2 on GNOME 50.3
(Bluefin 44) was tested by hand. Guest drags of files and text, and the
Escape after a guest to host drop, were then tested by hand with the
final code.
